      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 8: FAQ -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif; color: var(--text-primary); margin-bottom: 30px;">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
        
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What is the difference between adhesives and sealants?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Adhesives are high-strength polymers engineered to transmit mechanical loads between substrates, offering high shear and tensile strength. Sealants are lower-strength, high-elongation elastomeric networks designed to fill joints, accommodate thermal expansion, and block moisture or gas bypass.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. Why is surface energy matching important?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              For an adhesive to bond, it must wet the substrate's surface. According to thermodynamic wetting principles, the surface energy of the solid substrate must be higher than the surface tension of the liquid adhesive. Low-energy plastics (such as polyethylene) require plasma or corona treatments to introduce polar groups and enable bonding.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. What is the benefit of hybrid MS Polymers?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              MS Polymers (Silyl-Terminated Polyethers) combine the high UV and weathering resistance of silicones with the high strength, paintability, and broad substrate compatibility of polyurethanes. They cure via silanol condensation and are free of hazardous isocyanates, making them GHS-safe.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. How are adhesives compounded at industrial scale?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Adhesives are compounded using double-planetary vacuum mixers and high-shear dispersers equipped with heating and cooling jackets. The raw fillers are dehydrated under vacuum before catalysts are introduced at low temperatures, ensuring the formulation remains free of micro-air bubbles and premature curing.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. Where can I get custom adhesive formulations and engineering support?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Global Formulation provides complete chemical R&D, GHS-compliant formulations, raw material audits, and turnkey plant process designs. Specialist Absar Khan and our engineering team support factories worldwide in scaling up industrial and consumer adhesives.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

